D("progress",{etymology:E("From Old French progres and Latin progressus ‘a going forward’, from progredi ‘go forward’, formed from pro- ‘forward’ + gradi ‘step, walk’.","pro-","gradi","-ess","Etymonline, progress (https://www.etymonline.com/word/progress)."),contexts:[C("progress.s1","教育","The class made steady progress in reading after the teacher introduced short daily practice.","老師開始每日短時間練習後，全班閱讀能力穩步進步。","Progress is improvement or movement toward a goal or finished state.","Made progress is a common noun phrase; in reading identifies the skill.","The students’ ability improves over time."),C("progress.s2","通用","The convoy’s slow progress across the mountain pass worried the guide as the snow began to fall.","車隊在山口緩慢前進，降雪開始後讓嚮導感到擔心。","Progress can mean forward movement through a place or stages.","Progress is modified by slow and followed by across to show physical movement.","The vehicles are advancing slowly along a route."),C("progress.s3","通用","The negotiations are progressing faster now that both sides have agreed on the delivery dates.","雙方同意交貨日期後，談判現在進展得更快。","As a verb, progress means develop or move forward.","Progressing is the main verb; faster gives its rate.","The negotiations are advancing toward an agreement.")],mistakes:[M("countability","The students made a progress after every lesson and improved their reading each week.","The students made progress after every lesson and improved their reading each week.","Progress meaning improvement is generally uncountable and is not normally used with a here.")],quiz:[Q("progress.q1","progress.s1","meaning","If a student makes progress, the student ___.",["improves or moves toward a goal","moves backward in every skill","stops learning entirely"],"improves or moves toward a goal","Progress can mean improvement over time.","meaning"),Q("progress.q2","progress.s2","context","In “The slow progress of the convoy worried us,” progress means ___.",["forward movement along the route","better exam results","a computer application"],"forward movement along the route","The convoy is physically moving forward.","context"),Q("progress.q3","progress.s3","grammar","Choose the correct verb form: “The project is ____ well.”",["progressing","progress","progression"],"progressing","The progressive verb form is needed after is.","grammar"),Q("progress.q4","progress.s1","countability","Which phrase is standard?",["make good progress","make a good progress","make progresses much"],"make good progress","Progress as improvement is usually uncountable.","countability")]});
D("progression",{etymology:E("From Latin progressionem, from progressus ‘a going forward’, based on progredi ‘go forward’; the noun can describe development, a sequence, or a mathematical series.","pro-","gradi","-ion","Etymonline, progression (https://www.etymonline.com/word/progression)."),contexts:[C("progression.s1","醫學","The treatment slowed the progression of the disease, giving the patient more time to recover strength.","治療減緩了疾病的發展進程，讓病人有更多時間恢復體力。","Progression is steady movement or development over time.","Progression is followed by of and the process developing.","The disease advances more slowly under treatment."),C("progression.s2","職場","Her career progression took her from junior analyst to regional director over eight years.","八年間，她的職涯一路從初級分析師晉升到區域主管。","Progression can be a sequence of related stages or developments.","Progression is modified by career; from and to identify the sequence.","The jobs form an ordered series of increasing responsibility."),C("progression.s3","數學","The sequence 3, 6, 9, 12 is an arithmetic progression with a constant difference of three.","數列 3、6、9、12 是公差為三的等差數列。","In mathematics, a progression is a sequence of numbers following a rule.","Progression is the complement after is; with introduces its defining rule.","Each term increases by the same fixed amount.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The progression of the illness was a single unchanged moment with no development, stages, or movement over time.","The progression of the illness was its gradual development through successive stages.","Progression refers to forward movement or development, not a completely static condition.")],quiz:[Q("progression.q1","progression.s1","meaning","The progression of a disease is its ___.",["development over time","price in a store","outline from the side"],"development over time","Progression describes how a process advances or develops.","meaning"),Q("progression.q2","progression.s2","context","A career progression describes ___.",["a sequence of jobs or stages over time","a single unchanged job title","a list of personal expenses"],"a sequence of jobs or stages over time","The phrase focuses on successive career steps.","context"),Q("progression.q3","progression.s3","meaning","What makes 2, 4, 6, 8 an arithmetic progression?",["Each term increases by the same amount.","The numbers are all spelled alike.","The sequence has no order."],"Each term increases by the same amount.","An arithmetic progression uses a constant difference between terms.","meaning"),Q("progression.q4","progression.s2","word-choice","The report traced the gradual ____ of the company from a small shop to a national chain.",["progression","projectile","profligacy"],"progression","The company develops through successive stages.","word-choice")]});
D("progressive",{etymology:E("From progress + -ive or French progressif, from Latin progredi ‘go forward’; the adjective first described advancement and later acquired senses of gradual change and support for reform.","pro-","gradi","-ive","Etymonline, progressive (https://www.etymonline.com/word/progress)."),contexts:[C("progressive.s1","醫學","The patient experienced a progressive loss of hearing over several years, so her family sought specialist care.","病人幾年來聽力逐漸減退，因此家人尋求專科照護。","Progressive means developing slowly and step by step over time.","Progressive modifies loss; over several years gives its gradual duration.","The hearing decline becomes more pronounced across successive years."),C("progressive.s2","政治","The progressive council proposed reforms that expanded access to public transport and affordable housing.","進步派議會提出改革，擴大大眾運輸與平價住宅的可及性。","Progressive can mean supporting new ideas, reform, or social change.","Progressive modifies council; that clause describes its reform proposals.","The policies seek changes aimed at addressing social needs.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The progressive policy prevented any change and preserved every old rule exactly as it was.","The progressive policy introduced reforms and supported new approaches to public services.","Progressive in the reform sense favors change and new ideas, not blocking all change.")],quiz:[Q("progressive.q1","progressive.s1","meaning","A progressive illness gets worse ___.",["gradually over time","all at once and then never changes","only in the past"],"gradually over time","This sense describes a condition developing step by step.","meaning"),Q("progressive.q2","progressive.s2","context","A progressive reformer is likely to support ___.",["new ideas and social change","returning every policy to an unchanging past","banning all public discussion"],"new ideas and social change","The political sense favors reform or innovation.","context"),Q("progressive.q3","progressive.s1","word-choice","The illness caused a ____ decline in muscle strength over several months.",["progressive","prolific","profitless"],"progressive","The decline advances gradually over time.","word-choice"),Q("progressive.q4","progressive.s2","meaning","Does progressive always mean “gradual”?",["No; it can also mean favoring reform or social change.","Yes; it only describes a slope.","No; it means extremely expensive."],"No; it can also mean favoring reform or social change.","The adjective has both gradual-development and reform-oriented senses.","meaning")]});
D("progressively",{etymology:E("From progressive + -ly; progressive comes from progress and Latin progredi ‘go forward’, so the adverb describes change occurring in stages or becoming greater over time.","pro-","gradi","-ively","Etymonline, progressive (https://www.etymonline.com/word/progress)."),contexts:[C("progressively.s1","通用","The trail became progressively narrower as it climbed through the rocks toward the ridge.","步道穿過岩石往稜線上升時，逐漸變得越來越窄。","Progressively can mean in stages over time or to an increasing degree.","Progressively modifies narrower, a comparative adjective.","The path’s width decreases gradually as the hikers climb.") ,C("progressively.s2","通用","The tasks became progressively harder, with each level adding a new rule to learn.","任務變得越來越難，每一關都增加一條新規則。","Progressively before a comparative means more and more as time or stages pass.","Progressively modifies harder and the following clause explains the increasing difficulty.","Each stage adds complexity beyond the previous one.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The workload became progressively lighter at every stage, meaning each new assignment was harder than the one before.","The workload became progressively heavier at every stage, meaning each new assignment was harder than the one before.","Progressively heavier means increasing degree; lighter would describe the opposite direction.")],quiz:[Q("progressively.q1","progressively.s1","meaning","If a road becomes progressively steeper, it ___.",["gets steeper little by little","becomes flat immediately","stays exactly unchanged"],"gets steeper little by little","The adverb describes a gradual change over stages.","meaning"),Q("progressively.q2","progressively.s2","context","“The final rounds were progressively more difficult” means ___.",["each round was harder than the one before","the rounds became easier each time","all rounds had exactly the same difficulty"],"each round was harder than the one before","Progressively modifies the comparative and signals increasing degree.","context"),Q("progressively.q3","progressively.s2","grammar","Which phrase uses progressively with a comparative correctly?",["progressively more expensive","progressively expensiveest","progressively most expensive than"],"progressively more expensive","Progressively commonly modifies a comparative such as more expensive.","grammar"),Q("progressively.q4","progressively.s1","word-choice","The river became ____ shallow as the dry season continued.",["progressively","prohibitively","progenitor"],"progressively","The water depth decreased gradually over time.","word-choice")]});
D("prohibit",{etymology:E("From Latin prohibitus, past participle of prohibere ‘hold back, restrain, hinder’, from pro ‘away, forth’ + habere ‘hold’; the English verb means forbid officially or make impossible.","pro-","habere","-it","Etymonline, prohibit (https://www.etymonline.com/word/prohibit)."),contexts:[C("prohibit.s1","法律","The building rules prohibit residents from storing fuel in the shared stairwell.","大樓規章禁止住戶在共用樓梯間存放燃料。","To prohibit is to forbid something by an official rule or authority.","Prohibit takes residents from storing as its object pattern.","The rules formally disallow a potentially dangerous action."),C("prohibit.s2","通用","A lack of daylight prohibited the crew from finishing the outdoor survey before dusk.","缺乏日照使工作人員無法在黃昏前完成戶外調查。","To prohibit can mean make an action impossible or prevent it.","Prohibited takes the crew from finishing; lack of daylight is the cause.","The available light makes it impossible to complete the work on time.")],mistakes:[M("grammar","The city prohibited residents to park on the bridge during repairs.","The city prohibited residents from parking on the bridge during repairs.","Use prohibit someone from doing something, or prohibit an activity directly.")],quiz:[Q("prohibit.q1","prohibit.s1","meaning","If a rule prohibits an activity, it ___.",["officially forbids it","recommends it strongly","records it after it happens"],"officially forbids it","Prohibit means forbid by authority.","meaning"),Q("prohibit.q2","prohibit.s2","context","Which condition may prohibit a team from playing outdoors?",["A lightning storm makes the field unsafe.","The sky is clear and the field is open.","The team has plenty of time and equipment."],"A lightning storm makes the field unsafe.","The storm makes outdoor play impossible or unsafe.","context"),Q("prohibit.q3","prohibit.s1","grammar","Complete the standard pattern: “The policy prohibits staff ___ smoking indoors.”",["from","to","for"],"from","Prohibit someone from doing something is the standard construction.","grammar"),Q("prohibit.q4","prohibit.s1","word-choice","The law will ____ the sale of fireworks near the hospital.",["prohibit","progress","profile"],"prohibit","The law formally forbids that sale.","word-choice")]});
D("prohibition",{etymology:E("From Latin prohibitionem ‘a forbidding’, from prohibere ‘hold back, restrain’; the noun names an official order or act that prohibits something.","pro-","habere","-ition","Etymonline, prohibition (https://www.etymonline.com/word/prohibition)."),contexts:[C("prohibition.s1","法律","A prohibition on open fires remained in force throughout the dry month.","整個乾旱月份，露天生火的禁令持續有效。","A prohibition is an official order or rule forbidding an action.","Prohibition is followed by on and the activity being forbidden.","The rule prohibits open fires during a high-risk period.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The prohibition on parking allowed every vehicle to stay overnight wherever its driver chose.","The prohibition on parking forbade vehicles from staying in the restricted area overnight.","A prohibition is a ban, so it does not grant permission for the banned action.")],quiz:[Q("prohibition.q1","prohibition.s1","meaning","A prohibition on entry is ___.",["an official ban on entering","an invitation to enter","a map of the building"],"an official ban on entering","Prohibition names an order that forbids an action.","meaning"),Q("prohibition.q2","prohibition.s1","context","Which is a prohibition?",["A posted rule banning swimming in a dangerous reservoir","A sign welcoming visitors to swim","A schedule for a swimming lesson"],"A posted rule banning swimming in a dangerous reservoir","The posted ban forbids the activity.","context"),Q("prohibition.q3","prohibition.s1","collocation","Choose the natural phrase: “a prohibition ___ loud music after midnight.”",["on","at","from"],"on","Prohibition on is commonly used before a banned action.","collocation"),Q("prohibition.q4","prohibition.s1","word-choice","The council adopted a ____ against dumping waste in the river.",["prohibition","progression","profile"],"prohibition","The council formally bans the harmful activity.","word-choice")]});
D("prohibitive",{etymology:E("From Medieval Latin prohibitivus, from prohibit-, the stem of prohibere ‘hold back, forbid’; it first meant serving to forbid and later described a cost so high that it prevents action.","pro-","habere","-ive","Etymonline, prohibitive (https://www.etymonline.com/word/prohibitive)."),contexts:[C("prohibitive.s1","法律","The court upheld the prohibitive regulation that bars chemical dumping near the drinking-water source.","法院維持了禁止在飲用水水源附近傾倒化學物質的規定。","Prohibitive can mean intended or serving to forbid an action.","Prohibitive modifies regulation; that clause states what it bars.","The regulation legally prohibits dumping in a protected area."),C("prohibitive.s2","商業","The hotel’s prohibitive rates made it impossible for the team to stay there during the tournament.","飯店高得令人卻步的房價，使隊伍在比賽期間無法入住。","A prohibitive price is so high that it prevents people from buying or using something.","Prohibitive modifies rates; made it impossible states their effect.","The cost is beyond the team’s budget and blocks the booking.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The prohibitive ticket price was so low that every family could easily afford several seats.","The prohibitive ticket price was so high that many families could not afford even one seat.","Prohibitive in the cost sense means prohibitively high, not unusually cheap.")],quiz:[Q("prohibitive.q1","prohibitive.s1","meaning","A prohibitive rule is one that ___.",["forbids or prevents an action","encourages everyone to act","records an event afterward"],"forbids or prevents an action","The original adjective sense concerns forbidding.","meaning"),Q("prohibitive.q2","prohibitive.s2","context","Why might a fee be called prohibitive?",["It is so high that people cannot afford the service.","It is low enough for everyone.","It is paid only after a service is free."],"It is so high that people cannot afford the service.","A prohibitive cost stops people from buying or doing something.","context"),Q("prohibitive.q3","prohibitive.s2","word-choice","The ____ cost of replacing the roof forced the family to seek a cheaper plan.",["prohibitive","prolific","progressive"],"prohibitive","The cost is high enough to prevent the replacement choice.","word-choice"),Q("prohibitive.q4","prohibitive.s1","meaning","Which phrase uses prohibitive in its legal sense?",["a prohibitive order banning entry","a prohibitive hotel rate","a prohibitive fare for a flight"],"a prohibitive order banning entry","This use describes an order that forbids an action.","meaning")]});
D("prohibitively",{etymology:E("From prohibitive + -ly; prohibitive comes from Latin prohibere ‘hold back, forbid’, and the adverb commonly means so costly, difficult, or risky that it prevents action.","pro-","habere","-ively","Etymonline, prohibitive (https://www.etymonline.com/word/prohibitive)."),contexts:[C("prohibitively.s1","商業","The repairs were prohibitively expensive, so the museum closed the gallery until it could raise funds.","維修費高得令人卻步，因此博物館關閉了展廳，直到籌到經費為止。","Prohibitively means to such a high degree that it prevents an action.","Prohibitively modifies expensive and explains why the gallery closed.","The cost prevents the museum from proceeding with repairs immediately.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The permit fee was prohibitively low, so applicants could never afford to submit a form.","The permit fee was prohibitively high, so applicants could not afford to submit a form.","Prohibitively modifies a cost that is high enough to prevent action, not a low price.")],quiz:[Q("prohibitively.q1","prohibitively.s1","meaning","If a service is prohibitively expensive, it is ___.",["so costly that many people cannot use it","free of charge","slightly cheaper than expected"],"so costly that many people cannot use it","The adverb indicates a cost high enough to prevent use.","meaning"),Q("prohibitively.q2","prohibitively.s1","context","Why did the group abandon the project?",["The materials were prohibitively costly for its budget.","The materials were free and readily available.","The project required no materials."],"The materials were prohibitively costly for its budget.","The high cost prevented the group from continuing.","context"),Q("prohibitively.q3","prohibitively.s1","word-choice","Insurance premiums rose ____ after the building was damaged.",["prohibitively","profitably","progenitor"],"prohibitively","The rates became so high they prevented people from affording coverage.","word-choice"),Q("prohibitively.q4","prohibitively.s1","meaning","Which is closest to prohibitively difficult?",["so difficult that it prevents people from proceeding","easy for everyone to complete","slightly unusual but manageable"],"so difficult that it prevents people from proceeding","The adverb can describe a barrier of excessive difficulty as well as cost.","meaning")]});
D("project",{etymology:E("From Medieval Latin proiectum ‘something thrown forth’, from Latin proicere ‘throw forward’, pro- ‘forward’ + iacere ‘throw’; the noun became a plan, and the verb includes plan, protrude, estimate, or cast an image.","pro-","iacere","-ect","Etymonline, project (https://www.etymonline.com/word/project)."),contexts:[C("project.s1","教育","Our class project maps the local stream and tests its water quality at three points.","我們的班級專題繪製當地溪流地圖，並在三處檢測水質。","A project is a planned piece of work with a particular goal.","Project is the subject; maps and tests describe the planned work.","The class is carrying out an organized investigation to meet a learning goal."),C("project.s2","通用","The housing project will begin next spring after the city finishes reviewing the permits.","市府完成許可審查後，住宅計畫將於明年春季開工。","A project can be a proposal or planned scheme for future work.","Project is the subject; will begin marks its future start.","The housing development is an intended scheme awaiting approval."),C("project.s3","建築","The stone balcony projects over the narrow lane and shields pedestrians from rain.","石砌陽台突出在窄巷上方，替行人遮雨。","To project can mean stick out beyond an edge or surface.","Projects is an intransitive verb; over identifies what it extends above.","The balcony extends outward past the wall line."),C("project.s4","科技","The theater will project the restored film onto a wide screen during the festival.","影展期間，劇院將把修復後的影片投影到寬螢幕上。","To project can mean throw an image onto a surface; it can also mean estimate a future figure or present an image of oneself.","Project takes the film as object; onto gives the receiving surface.","The image is cast onto the theater screen.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The balcony projected inward behind the wall, so it extended outward over the street and sheltered passersby.","The balcony projected outward over the street, extending beyond the wall and sheltering passersby.","Project in the protrusion sense means extend outward beyond an edge, not inward behind it.")],quiz:[Q("project.q1","project.s1","meaning","A science project is ___.",["planned work with a particular goal","a random event with no plan","a machine that only displays films"],"planned work with a particular goal","The noun names organized work undertaken for an aim.","meaning"),Q("project.q2","project.s2","context","A city’s housing project is ___.",["a planned development proposal","an old movie already screened","a side view of a building"],"a planned development proposal","The phrase names a planned scheme for construction.","context"),Q("project.q3","project.s3","meaning","If a ledge projects beyond a wall, it ___.",["sticks out past the wall’s edge","is hidden entirely inside the wall","is shown on a movie screen"],"sticks out past the wall’s edge","This intransitive sense means protrude or extend outward.","meaning"),Q("project.q4","project.s4","context","In a cinema, to project a film is to ___.",["cast its images onto a screen","calculate its ticket revenue","write a review before watching it"],"cast its images onto a screen","The verb means display the film image on a surface.","context")]});
D("projectile",{etymology:E("From Medieval Latin projectilis ‘capable of being thrown’, from Latin proiectus, past participle of proicere ‘throw forth’; -ile forms an adjective, later used as a noun for a thrown or fired object.","pro-","iacere","-ile","Etymonline, projectile (https://www.etymonline.com/word/projectile)."),contexts:[C("projectile.s1","物理","The engineer calculated how far the projectile would travel after leaving the launcher at an angle.","工程師計算彈體以一定角度離開發射器後能飛多遠。","A projectile is an object thrown or fired forward through the air.","Projectile is the subject of would travel; after identifies its launch.","The object follows a path through the air after being propelled.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The projectile remained bolted to the launcher and never moved through the air after firing.","The projectile left the launcher and traveled through the air toward the target.","A projectile is the object that is thrown or fired forward, not an object fixed in place.")],quiz:[Q("projectile.q1","projectile.s1","meaning","A projectile is an object that is ___.",["thrown or fired through the air","painted onto a wall","used to list prices"],"thrown or fired through the air","The noun refers to something propelled forward.","meaning"),Q("projectile.q2","projectile.s1","context","Which item is a projectile in a physics experiment?",["A ball launched from a ramp","The stationary table supporting the ramp","The written lab instructions"],"A ball launched from a ramp","The moving ball travels through the air after launch.","context"),Q("projectile.q3","projectile.s1","word-choice","The cannon sent a ____ toward the distant target.",["projectile","progression","programme"],"projectile","The fired object is a projectile.","word-choice"),Q("projectile.q4","projectile.s1","meaning","What happens to a projectile after launch?",["It moves through the air under the forces acting on it.","It automatically becomes a television show.","It stays permanently attached to the cannon."],"It moves through the air under the forces acting on it.","A projectile is defined by being thrown or fired forward.","meaning")]});
D("projecting",{etymology:E("The present participle of project, from Latin proicere ‘throw forward’; as an adjective in this source entry, it describes a part that sticks out beyond a surface or edge.","pro-","iacere","-ing","Etymonline, project (https://www.etymonline.com/word/project)."),contexts:[C("projecting.s1","建築","A projecting stone above the doorway caught the rain and kept it from dripping onto the steps.","門上方突出的石檐接住雨水，避免雨滴落在台階上。","Projecting means sticking out beyond a surface or edge.","Projecting modifies stone; above locates it relative to the doorway.","The stone extends past the wall surface to form an overhang.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The projecting beam was completely flush with the wall and did not extend beyond any surface.","The projecting beam extended beyond the wall and partly blocked the doorway.","Projecting describes something that sticks out; a flush beam does not project.")],quiz:[Q("projecting.q1","projecting.s1","meaning","A projecting branch ___.",["sticks out beyond the trunk or another surface","grows entirely inside the trunk","is a computer program"],"sticks out beyond the trunk or another surface","Projecting means extending outward beyond an edge.","meaning"),Q("projecting.q2","projecting.s1","context","Which object is projecting?",["A shelf that extends several inches beyond the wall","A flat picture mounted flush to the wall","A cable hidden inside a pipe"],"A shelf that extends several inches beyond the wall","The shelf protrudes beyond the wall surface.","context"),Q("projecting.q3","projecting.s1","word-choice","The worker padded a ____ nail that stuck out from the board.",["projecting","profuse","progressive"],"projecting","The nail extends beyond the board and can cause injury.","word-choice"),Q("projecting.q4","projecting.s1","meaning","What is the opposite of a projecting ledge?",["a ledge flush with the wall","a ledge extending into the room","a ledge that casts a shadow"],"a ledge flush with the wall","A flush ledge does not stick out beyond the wall.","meaning")]});
D("projection",{etymology:E("From Latin projectionem ‘a throwing forward’, from projectus, past participle of proicere ‘throw forth’; modern senses include a forecast and an image or map cast onto a surface.","pro-","iacere","-ion","Etymonline, projection (https://www.etymonline.com/word/projection)."),contexts:[C("projection.s1","商業","The sales projection assumed a slower summer season after two large customers delayed their orders.","兩家大客戶延後訂單後，銷售預測假設夏季業績會較慢。","A projection is an estimate of a future amount or change.","Projection is the subject; assumed describes its underlying forecast.","The figure estimates sales ahead using current evidence."),C("projection.s2","科學","The map projection stretches the polar regions because it transfers a curved globe onto a flat page.","地圖投影把球面轉到平面紙張上，因此拉伸了極地區域。","Projection can be an image or representation produced by projecting, including a map transformation.","Projection is the subject; because explains the distortion.","A curved surface cannot be flattened without changing some distances or shapes.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The sales projection was the exact amount already collected last year, not an estimate of a future amount.","The sales projection was an estimate of the amount the company expected to sell next year.","Projection in business forecasting means an estimate about the future, not a record of past actual sales.")],quiz:[Q("projection.q1","projection.s1","meaning","A sales projection is ___.",["an estimate of future sales","a receipt for a completed sale","a projector used in a theater"],"an estimate of future sales","Projection can name a forecast of a future amount.","meaning"),Q("projection.q2","projection.s2","context","Why can a map projection distort Greenland?",["It represents a curved globe on a flat surface.","It changes the country’s actual size overnight.","It predicts future weather over the island."],"It represents a curved globe on a flat surface.","Flattening a globe into a map changes shapes or distances.","context"),Q("projection.q3","projection.s1","word-choice","The budget includes a five-year revenue ____ based on current orders.",["projection","projectile","profligacy"],"projection","It estimates future revenue from present information.","word-choice"),Q("projection.q4","projection.s2","meaning","Which use is a projection of light?",["An image cast onto a wall by a projector","A forecast of next year’s sales","A stone ledge extending from a wall"],"An image cast onto a wall by a projector","Projection also names an image or pattern thrown onto a surface.","meaning")]});
D("projectionist",{etymology:E("Formed from projection + -ist, a suffix naming a person associated with an activity; in cinema, a projectionist operates a projector during a screening.",null,"project","-ionist","Etymonline, projection (https://www.etymonline.com/word/projection)."),contexts:[C("projectionist.s1","電影","The projectionist checked the focus before the audience entered the restored theater.","觀眾進入修復後的劇院前，放映員先檢查了焦距。","A projectionist is a person who operates a projector during a film screening.","Projectionist is the subject; before gives the timing of the check.","The worker prepares the projection equipment for the screening.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The projectionist sold popcorn in the lobby but never operated or checked any projection equipment.","The projectionist operated the film projector and checked its focus before the show.","A projectionist’s defining work is operating projection equipment, not merely selling concessions.")],quiz:[Q("projectionist.q1","projectionist.s1","meaning","A cinema projectionist operates ___.",["the projector showing the film","the ticket printer only","the audience’s seats"],"the projector showing the film","The projectionist runs the equipment that displays the movie.","meaning"),Q("projectionist.q2","projectionist.s1","context","Who checks whether a movie is in focus before a screening?",["The projectionist","The ticket buyer","The person who wrote the film review"],"The projectionist","Checking and running the projection system is the projectionist’s job.","context"),Q("projectionist.q3","projectionist.s1","word-choice","The ____ started the reel when the theater lights dimmed.",["projectionist","progenitor","prohibitive"],"projectionist","The worker starts the equipment that shows the film.","word-choice"),Q("projectionist.q4","projectionist.s1","meaning","Which is the projectionist’s main task?",["Show a film by operating projection equipment","Forecast next year’s ticket sales","Write a program for the theater website"],"Show a film by operating projection equipment","The job is to run the projector during a screening.","meaning")]});
D("projector",{etymology:E("From project + -or, an agent or instrument suffix; project derives from Latin proicere ‘throw forward’, and a projector throws images onto a screen.","pro-","iacere","-or","Etymonline, project (https://www.etymonline.com/word/project)."),contexts:[C("projector.s1","科技","Connect the laptop to the projector so the team can view the design on the large screen.","把筆記型電腦接上投影機，讓團隊能在大螢幕上查看設計。","A projector is a machine that displays images or films on a screen.","Projector is the object of connect; so introduces the viewing purpose.","The device casts the laptop’s image onto a large display."),C("projector.s2","教育","The teacher used an old slide projector to show photographs from the field trip.","老師用一台舊幻燈機播放校外教學的照片。","A projector can specifically be an older machine for displaying photographic slides.","Slide projector is the full name; used to introduces its function.","The device projects individual slides rather than a digital presentation.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The projector was the person who sold tickets while the machine merely operated the projector.","The projectionist sold or managed the screening, while the projector was the machine displaying the images.","Projector names the machine; projectionist names the person who operates it.")],quiz:[Q("projector.q1","projector.s1","meaning","A classroom projector is used to ___.",["display images on a screen","record attendance by itself","measure the room’s temperature"],"display images on a screen","A projector casts a visual image onto a surface.","meaning"),Q("projector.q2","projector.s2","context","What does a slide projector display?",["Photographic slides","Computer code as sound","Printed price lists only"],"Photographic slides","The older device shows individual slides.","context"),Q("projector.q3","projector.s1","word-choice","The speaker connected her computer to the ____ before the presentation.",["projector","projectionist","projectile"],"projector","The device displays the computer image for the audience.","word-choice"),Q("projector.q4","projector.s1","meaning","What is the difference between a projector and a projectionist?",["The projector is a machine; the projectionist is a person who operates it.","Both words mean the same machine.","The projectionist is an image shown on a screen."],"The projector is a machine; the projectionist is a person who operates it.","The -ist form names a person associated with projection.","meaning")]});
D("proliferate",{etymology:E("From French proliférer, from Latin prolifer ‘offspring-bearing’, built from proles ‘offspring’ + ferre ‘bear, carry’; the verb means grow or cause to grow rapidly in number.","pro-","proles + ferre","-ate","Etymonline, proliferation (https://www.etymonline.com/word/proliferation)."),contexts:[C("proliferate.s1","通用","Small repair shops proliferated across the district after the new transit line brought more customers.","新交通線帶來更多顧客後，小型修理店在整個區域迅速增加。","To proliferate can mean increase quickly in number and spread widely.","Proliferated is intransitive; shops are the subject and across marks the spread.","Many new shops appear throughout the district."),C("proliferate.s2","科技","The platform proliferated low-cost training videos by making them easy for instructors to upload.","這個平台讓講師容易上傳低成本訓練影片，因而使這類影片大量增加。","To proliferate can also mean cause something to increase rapidly in number.","Proliferated is transitive here and takes videos as its object.","The platform enables a rapid increase in the number of videos.")],mistakes:[M("grammar","The number of pop-up shops proliferated them across the district after the new station opened.","Pop-up shops proliferated across the district after the new station opened.","In the intransitive sense, proliferate does not take an object; use a transitive construction only when an agent causes something to multiply.")],quiz:[Q("proliferate.q1","proliferate.s1","meaning","When rumors proliferate, they ___.",["increase rapidly and spread","disappear completely","become a single verified fact"],"increase rapidly and spread","The intransitive verb means grow quickly in number.","meaning"),Q("proliferate.q2","proliferate.s2","context","A new app proliferates online tutorials when it ___.",["causes many more tutorials to be created or shared","deletes every tutorial","prevents users from uploading anything"],"causes many more tutorials to be created or shared","This transitive sense means cause a rapid increase.","context"),Q("proliferate.q3","proliferate.s1","grammar","Which sentence uses proliferate intransitively?",["The weeds proliferated in the empty lot.","The weeds proliferated the gardener into leaving.","The gardener proliferated of the weeds."],"The weeds proliferated in the empty lot.","The subject weeds increase in number without a direct object.","grammar"),Q("proliferate.q4","proliferate.s1","word-choice","After the rumor spread online, false copies began to ____ across several sites.",["proliferate","prohibit","project"],"proliferate","The copies increase quickly and appear widely.","word-choice")]});
D("proliferation",{etymology:E("From French prolifération, from prolifère and Latin proles ‘offspring’ + ferre ‘bear’; it first named cell growth and broadened to rapid increase or spread in number.","pro-","proles + ferre","-ation","Etymonline, proliferation (https://www.etymonline.com/word/proliferation)."),contexts:[C("proliferation.s1","政治","The treaty was designed to limit the proliferation of nuclear weapons by strengthening inspections.","該條約透過加強檢查，旨在限制核武器擴散。","Proliferation is rapid growth or increase in number, especially of cells, weapons, or similar items.","Proliferation is followed by of and the items increasing.","Inspections aim to keep the number of weapons from spreading to more states.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The proliferation of clinics meant the number of clinics steadily fell until only one remained.","The proliferation of clinics meant that many new clinics appeared across the region.","Proliferation is an increase in number, not a decline.")],quiz:[Q("proliferation.q1","proliferation.s1","meaning","A proliferation of devices is ___.",["a rapid increase in their number","a complete disappearance of them","a single device being repaired"],"a rapid increase in their number","The noun describes rapid growth or spread.","meaning"),Q("proliferation.q2","proliferation.s1","context","In international policy, nuclear proliferation concerns ___.",["the spread or increase of nuclear weapons","the repair of a television screen","the order of a concert program"],"the spread or increase of nuclear weapons","The term is commonly used for growth in the number of nuclear weapons or states possessing them.","context"),Q("proliferation.q3","proliferation.s1","collocation","Complete the common phrase: “the proliferation ___ mobile devices.”",["of","at","for"],"of","Proliferation is commonly followed by of and the items increasing.","collocation"),Q("proliferation.q4","proliferation.s1","meaning","What does a rise in cell proliferation indicate?",["Cells are increasing through growth or division.","Cells have stopped existing.","The cells have turned into a printed program."],"Cells are increasing through growth or division.","In biology, proliferation names rapid cell production.","meaning")]});
D("proliferous",{etymology:E("From Latin prolifer ‘offspring-bearing’ (proles ‘offspring’ + ferre ‘bear’) + -ous; in biology it describes a plant or organism producing abundant new growth or outgrowths.","pro-","proles + ferre","-ous","Etymonline, proliferation (https://www.etymonline.com/word/proliferation)."),contexts:[C("proliferous.s1","植物學","The proliferous strawberry plant sent out so many runners that its new shoots filled the garden bed.","這株增生旺盛的草莓長出許多匍匐莖，新芽很快填滿了花圃。","Proliferous means producing many new individuals or outgrowths.","Proliferous modifies plant; that clause explains the numerous offshoots.","The plant generates many new shoots through runners.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The proliferous fern produced no new fronds or spores during the growing season and remained unchanged.","The proliferous fern produced many new fronds and spores during the growing season.","Proliferous describes abundant new growth or reproduction, not an absence of it.")],quiz:[Q("proliferous.q1","proliferous.s1","meaning","A proliferous plant produces ___.",["many new shoots or growths","no growth at all","only one dry leaf forever"],"many new shoots or growths","The adjective describes plentiful new growth.","meaning"),Q("proliferous.q2","proliferous.s1","context","Which plant is proliferous?",["One that sends out numerous new shoots","One that has no buds, roots, or seeds","One that is made of metal"],"One that sends out numerous new shoots","Many offshoots fit the biological sense.","context"),Q("proliferous.q3","proliferous.s1","word-choice","The botanist recorded a ____ fern whose runners produced dozens of young plants.",["proliferous","prohibitively","progressive"],"proliferous","The fern produces many new plants through its runners.","word-choice"),Q("proliferous.q4","proliferous.s1","meaning","Is proliferous a common everyday substitute for prolific?",["No; it is a rarer biological adjective for abundant new growth.","Yes; the words are identical in every context.","No; it means impossible to reproduce."],"No; it is a rarer biological adjective for abundant new growth.","Proliferous is uncommon and has a specific growth sense.","meaning")]});
D("prolific",{etymology:E("From French prolifique and Medieval Latin prolificus, from Latin proles ‘offspring’ + facere ‘make, produce’; it describes abundant production of work, young, or fruit.","pro-","proles + facere","-ic","Etymonline, prolific (https://www.etymonline.com/word/prolific)."),contexts:[C("prolific.s1","文學","The prolific novelist published four mysteries in one year while also teaching a weekly writing class.","這位多產小說家一年出版四本懸疑小說，同時每週還開設寫作課。","Prolific can describe someone who produces many works.","Prolific modifies novelist; the rest specifies her frequent output.","Four books in one year show a high rate of creative production."),C("prolific.s2","生物","The prolific rabbit pair produced several litters, so the farm built a larger sheltered enclosure.","這對繁殖力強的兔子生了好幾胎，因此農場建了更大的遮蔽圍欄。","Prolific can describe animals or plants that produce many young or fruits.","Prolific modifies pair; produced gives evidence of their reproductive output.","Several litters are many offspring in a short period.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The prolific author published no books, stories, or articles throughout a forty-year career.","The prolific author published many books, stories, and articles throughout a forty-year career.","Prolific means producing a large amount of work or offspring, not producing none.")],quiz:[Q("prolific.q1","prolific.s1","meaning","A prolific writer ___.",["produces many works","writes only one sentence in a lifetime","refuses to publish anything"],"produces many works","Prolific means producing many books or other works.","meaning"),Q("prolific.q2","prolific.s2","context","Which animal is prolific?",["A rabbit that produces many litters","A sterile animal that produces no young","A stone statue in a garden"],"A rabbit that produces many litters","The animal has many offspring.","context"),Q("prolific.q3","prolific.s1","word-choice","The ____ composer wrote hundreds of songs over several decades.",["prolific","prohibitive","profound"],"prolific","The composer produced an unusually large body of work.","word-choice"),Q("prolific.q4","prolific.s2","meaning","In biology, a prolific plant is one that ___.",["produces abundant fruit or offspring","never grows or reproduces","has a very high price"],"produces abundant fruit or offspring","The biological sense concerns abundant production.","meaning")]});
